 "literal": "Having scooped up and taken from the long-sounding ocean expanse / the clouds moving in a gathered mass, to desired places having gathered, / appearing like a great mountain, the pregnancy maturing, / with thunder-roaring cloud-tools, having discharged their duty of rain, / on the abundant mountains, when the hot season has ceased, / all mankind going to know and experience, the Ganges / dashing against the bank, with abundant water filled and appearing, like that, / to us and to others, because you are an eminent lord, / `Without love, men having killed and plundered the path, / those who come forward are not they; without love, / to those who traversed the difficult desert path where strong male deer bellow, / with this day, a year passes` saying, / her eyes having lost brightness, with weeping, her strength perishing, / suffering difficult grief, my greatly suffering wife, your / effort-born wealth whenever seeing, to be amazed, / with a large trunk resembling a palmyra tree, producing pearls, matured, / tall tusks raised, resembling a mountain, the strong male elephant, / with a bright shining faceplate, to shine on its sides, / sounding bells ringing repeatedly, having mounted, proudly / desiring to go, I have attained, O victorious lord! / Poverty driving me, fame giving, having come, your / generosity-connected my desire, please listen. / Even if I am capable, or even if I am not capable, quickly / without considering and measuring my worth, / please know and measure your own excellent worth, O lord, always! / So that other kings feel ashamed, I will return, smearing sandalwood paste, / on my chest of prominent beauty with many marks, / whenever glorious jeweled women embrace, to delight, / where the morning drum sounds in the spacious territory, your / foot-shade dwellers' good ornaments abound, / with sword-battles exalted, your army, / and your glorious wealth, may we receive many indeed!",
 "translation": "The clouds scoop up water from the roaring ocean, gather in dark masses like great mountains, and discharge their heavy rain. When the rains cease on the high hills and the hot season begins, the Ganges floods, overflowing its banks with abundant water for all mankind to see. Just like that river, you are a source of life and abundance to us and to others, O Kumaṇaṉ! My wife, suffering in deep grief, her eyes dimmed with tears and her strength gone, laments: 'He has gone across the harsh desert where wild stags bellow and heartless bandits kill and plunder travelers. Today marks a full year since he left.' To amaze her when she sees the wealth born of your effort, I have come with a desire to ride back proudly on a mountain-like elephant with a trunk as thick as a palmyra tree, bearing mature tusks that yield pearls, adorned with a shining faceplate and ringing bells. Driven by poverty, drawn by your fame, I have come. Please listen to my plea, which is bound to your generosity. Do not measure my worth or capability; instead, measure your own boundless greatness and give. Let me return in such state that other kings feel ashamed, my chest smeared with sandalwood and marked by the close embraces of my jeweled wife. May your sword-wielding army and your glorious wealth flourish forever under your protective shade!",
 "modern": "கடலில் முகந்த நீரை முகில்கள் பெருமலை போலத் திரண்டு மழையாகப் பொழிந்து ஓய்ந்த பின், கங்கையாறு கரையை மோதிப் பெருகி ஓடுவது போல, நீ எமக்கும் பிறருக்கும் கொடையளிக்கும் வள்ளலாக விளங்குகிறாய். 'வழியில் செல்லும் மக்களைக் கொன்று கொள்ளையடிக்கும் அன்பற்ற ஆறலைப்பறவைகளைக் கடந்து, ஆண்மான்கள் கத்தும் அரிய பாலைவனத்தைக் கடந்து சென்ற என் கணவர் பிரிந்து இன்றோடு ஓராண்டு முடிகிறது' என்று அழுது கண்கள் ஒளி இழந்து வாடும் என் மனைவி, உனது கொடையைக் கண்டு வியக்கும்படி செய்ய விரும்புகிறேன். பனைமரம் போன்ற தும்பிக்கையையும், முத்துக்கள் விளையும் கொம்புகளையும் உடைய, நெற்றிப்பட்டமும் மணிகளும் அணிந்த யானையின் மீது ஏறிப் பெருமிதத்துடன் செல்ல விரும்புகிறேன். வறுமை துரத்த, உனது புகழைக் கேட்டு வந்துள்ள என் விருப்பத்தைக் கேட்பாயாக. எனது தகுதியை ஆராயாமல், உனது கொடைத்தன்மையின் அளவை அறிந்து எனக்குக் கொடுப்பாயாக. பிற மன்னர்கள் நாணமடையுமாறு, சந்தனம் பூசிய மார்புடன் நான் திரும்பிச் செல்ல வேண்டும். உனது குடைநிழலில் வாழ்வோர் சிறப்படைய, உனது வாள்வீரமும் செல்வமும் என்றும் சிறந்து விளங்குக!",
 "explanation": "The poet Peruñcittiraṉār addresses the patron Kumaṇaṉ in this Pāṭāṇ-tiṇai poem under the theme of Parisil (seeking gifts). He compares Kumaṇaṉ's boundless generosity to the flooding Ganges, which sustains all living beings after the monsoon rains have ceased. The poet describes the pathetic state of his wife, who is wasting away in grief, counting the days and realizing a full year has passed since he left to traverse the dangerous, bandit-ridden desert (suram). He requests Kumaṇaṉ to bestow a grand elephant and riches, not based on the poet's own limited worth (en aḷantu), but based on Kumaṇaṉ's own magnificent stature (nin aḷantu). This grand gift will not only relieve his wife's suffering but also make rival kings feel ashamed of their own smallness when the poet returns in triumph.",
